---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Deployment modes and Installer" activeNav="/deployment-modes">
  <h1>Deployment modes and Installer</h1>
  <p>
    An App Manager deployment has three independent decisions: how the first account is created,
    where credential material is stored, and whether secret material is exposed to authenticated
    API clients. Keeping those choices separate lets an interactive installation use an external
    vault, or an automatically provisioned installation keep secrets available only in the web UI.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuration at a glance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Decision</th><th>Variable</th><th>Values</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>First account</td>
        <td><code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP</code></td>
        <td><code>installer</code> (default), <code>admin</code>, or <code>demo</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Credential storage</td>
        <td><code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES</code></td>
        <td><code>local,external</code> (default), <code>local</code>, or <code>external</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Credential API</td>
        <td><code>CREDENTIALS_API_PROFILE</code></td>
        <td><code>full</code> (default) or <code>dashboard_only</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These variables are read when the application boots. With Compose, edit <code>.env</code> and
    run <code>docker compose up -d</code> so all application containers are recreated with the same
    configuration.
  </p>

  <h2>Credential storage modes</h2>
  <h3>Local: App Manager stores the secret</h3>
  <p>
    With <code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=local</code>, secure fields are encrypted in PostgreSQL and
    credential files are application-encrypted before being written to the configured private media
    store. “Local” describes who owns the secret, not necessarily the physical filesystem:
    <code>MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER=local</code> uses the persistent <code>storage-app</code> volume, while
    <code>MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER=s3</code> uses a private S3-compatible bucket.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="dotenv"
    code={`CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=local
CREDENTIALS_API_PROFILE=full

# Where locally managed credential files and other private media are stored:
MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER=local`}
  />
  <p>
    This is the fully functional App Manager-managed vault: authorized API clients can prepare
    signing material for builds, and the web dashboard can manage the same credentials. Keep
    <code>APP_KEY</code> stable and back it up with the database and media store.
  </p>

  <h3>External: bring your own vault</h3>
  <p>
    With <code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=external</code>, App Manager stores a provider, resource
    reference, and optional non-secret metadata. It does not hold credentials for the external
    vault, resolve the reference, or copy the referenced secret. Your build runner fetches the
    secret directly and can report whether resolution succeeded with
    <a href="/cli/credentials"><code>appmctl credentials verify</code></a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Supported reference types are AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault, Google
    Secret Manager, fastlane match, and a custom provider. See
    <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">Credentials Vault</a> for resource formats and the
    verification lifecycle.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="dotenv"
    code={`CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=external
# This controls the API surface; it does not make App Manager read the external vault.
CREDENTIALS_API_PROFILE=full`}
  />

  <h3>Both: let a team choose</h3>
  <p>
    <code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=local,external</code> is the default. It is the instance-wide
    ceiling: an owner can narrow a workspace to one of those modes under <strong>Team access →
    Permitted credential storage</strong>, but a workspace cannot enable a mode the instance has
    excluded. A credential's mode cannot be changed after creation; create a replacement and
    reattach it when moving between vaults.
  </p>

  <h2>Credential API profiles</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Profile</th><th>API behavior</th><th>Web dashboard</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>full</code></td>
        <td>Credential metadata, verification, payload reveal, file upload/download/delete, and Expo credential provisioning routes are available subject to API-key scopes and team permissions.</td>
        <td>Full credential management.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>dashboard_only</code></td>
        <td>Routes that reveal, upload, download, delete, or send secret material to Expo are not registered. Creating a local credential or changing its payload/file through the API is rejected. Metadata CRUD, external-reference CRUD, and verification remain available. New API keys cannot receive <code>credentials:reveal</code>.</td>
        <td>Authenticated users keep the normal credential UI, including local credential downloads.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    In <code>dashboard_only</code>, credential webhook and notification events are also suppressed so
    credential activity does not leave through those channels. Other event types continue to work.
    The profile is resolved at application boot, so restart every web, queue, and scheduler container
    after changing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Dashboard only” is an API security profile, not a read-only application mode. It can be paired
    with <code>local</code>, <code>external</code>, or both storage modes.
  </p>

  <h2>Recommended combinations</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Goal</th><th>Storage modes</th><th>API profile</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>App Manager is the complete credential vault for CI</td><td><code>local</code></td><td><code>full</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>All secrets remain in an external manager</td><td><code>external</code></td><td><code>full</code> or <code>dashboard_only</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Teams decide per credential</td><td><code>local,external</code></td><td><code>full</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets may be downloaded only by signed-in dashboard users</td><td><code>local</code></td><td><code>dashboard_only</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inventory and verify external references through the API, never transfer secrets</td><td><code>external</code></td><td><code>dashboard_only</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>First-run bootstrap modes</h2>
  <p>
    In the published Compose stack, the one-shot <code>init</code> service waits for PostgreSQL and
    Redis, runs database migrations, runs <code>encryption:migrate</code>, and finally executes
    <code>php artisan app:bootstrap</code>. The long-running app, queue, and scheduler services start
    only after <code>init</code> succeeds.
  </p>

  <h3>Web Installer (default)</h3>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="dotenv"
    code={`APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=installer
APP_KEY=base64:replace-with-a-generated-key
ALLOW_PUBLIC_REGISTRATION=false`}
  />
  <p>
    Installer mode does not create database records during <code>app:bootstrap</code>. Instead, it
    makes <code>/install</code> the only usable application screen until setup is complete. Normal
    browser GET requests redirect there; API and non-GET requests return <code>503</code> with the
    installer URL.
  </p>

  <h4>What the Installer checks</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>PHP 8.4 or newer and the OpenSSL extension;</li>
    <li>a working PDO database connection and the required migrated tables;</li>
    <li>a configured application encryption key;</li>
    <li>writable application and framework storage directories.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What the Installer configures</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>the instance name, first owner account, owner team, and current team;</li>
    <li>local private media storage or an S3-compatible bucket;</li>
    <li>optional GitHub App, GitLab OAuth, and outbound webhook integrations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Before committing anything, it tests write, read, and delete access to the selected media
    storage. S3 access keys are optional when the runtime supplies credentials through an IAM role;
    when supplied explicitly, the access key and secret key must be provided together. GitHub and
    GitLab secrets are required only when that provider is enabled.
  </p>
  <p>
    The owner, team, and installation record are created in one transaction. Instance settings and
    any supplied integration or S3 credentials are encrypted with <code>APP_KEY</code>. The owner is
    signed in and sent to the Integrations page after a successful installation.
  </p>

  <h4>One-time safety</h4>
  <p>
    Installer submissions are rate-limited and serialized with a lock. As soon as the installation
    record exists, <code>/install</code> redirects away. The presence of any user also closes it, so
    an older populated database cannot be taken over by reopening the setup URL. Public registration
    remains disabled unless <code>ALLOW_PUBLIC_REGISTRATION=true</code> is explicitly set.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep <code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=installer</code> after installation. You do not need to set
    <code>SELF_HOSTED_INSTALLER_ENABLED=false</code>: the installation record performs the lockout,
    while installer mode also ensures the encrypted installation settings are applied on later
    requests.
  </p>

  <h3>Admin from environment variables</h3>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="dotenv"
    code={`APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=admin
ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-strong-password
ADMIN_NAME=Owner
ADMIN_TEAM_NAME=My Team`}
  />
  <p>
    Admin mode is intended for automated or headless provisioning. <code>ADMIN_EMAIL</code> and
    <code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> are required on an empty database; the password is validated by the
    same rules as an account created through the UI. The name defaults to <code>Owner</code> and the
    team name to <code>My Team</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The command is idempotent at the instance level: if any user exists, it creates nothing and
    succeeds without requiring the <code>ADMIN_*</code> values. Remove <code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>
    from the deployment environment after the first successful start, but leave
    <code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=admin</code>. This mode does not create an installation-settings
    record; configure media storage and integrations with their environment variables.
  </p>

  <h3>Demo bootstrap</h3>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="dotenv"
    code={`APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=demo
APP_ENV=local`}
  />
  <p>
    Demo mode is deliberately rejected outside <code>local</code>, <code>development</code>, and
    <code>testing</code>. It creates the demo owner <code>demo@example.com</code> with password
    <code>Demo123!</code>, team slug <code>demo-users-team</code>, sample app
    <code>example-app</code>, and API key <code>demo-api-key</code>, plus Android/iOS identifiers,
    profiles, assets, integrations, and signing fixtures. Those certificates, profiles, keys, and
    service accounts are structurally useful test data but are not trusted by Apple, Google, or
    application stores.
  </p>
  <p>
    The container may generate a disposable <code>APP_KEY</code> for this mode only and persist it in
    the <code>storage-app</code> volume. Demo bootstrap skips seeding when any user already exists.
  </p>

  <h3>Database seed for repository development</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`cd apps/platform
php artisan db:seed`} />
  <p>
    The default database seeder is also limited to local, development, and testing environments. It
    creates <code>test@example.com</code> with password <code>password</code>, then invokes
    <code>DemoSeeder</code> to add the complete demo workspace. This differs from
    <code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=demo</code>, which calls only <code>DemoSeeder</code> and therefore
    does not create the test account. Both seeders are idempotent, but their credentials must never
    be exposed on an internet-facing instance.
  </p>

  <h2>Changing modes later</h2>
  <p>
    Storage-mode and API-profile changes affect what can be created or accessed after the next
    restart; they do not migrate existing credentials. A narrower storage ceiling can leave existing
    credentials visible while preventing new credentials in that mode. Plan migrations by creating
    replacements and reattaching build profiles before removing the old credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bootstrap modes are first-run mechanisms, not account migration tools. Do not switch a completed
    Installer deployment to <code>admin</code> or <code>demo</code>. For ongoing account management,
    invite team members or use the authenticated dashboard.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
